          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In the clinical scenario, the surgeon must precisely identify Calot's triangle d</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the clinical scenario, the surgeon must precisely identify Calot's triangle during cholecystectomy because it contains which critical vessel?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cystic artery</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The cystic artery runs within Calot's triangle and must be identified before ligation. Mistaking the right hepatic artery for the cystic artery during cholecystectomy causes right lobe ischaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Right hepatic artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. While the right hepatic artery is nearby and its accidental injury is the feared complication, the target vessel within Calot's triangle is the cystic artery — a branch of the right hepatic artery that supplies the gallbladder.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Common hepatic artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The common hepatic artery is a branch of the coeliac trunk and lies outside Calot's triangle. The vessel found within this triangle is the cystic artery, which must be clipped before gallbladder removal.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Gastroduodenal artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The gastroduodenal artery lies behind the first part of the duodenum, not within Calot's triangle. The cystic artery is the vessel contained in this triangular surgical landmark.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the narration: the surgeon must identify the cystic artery, which lies within Calot's triangle (hepatocystic triangle), before clipping the gallbladder's blood supply."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Identifying the cystic artery within Calot's triangle is the critical safety step in every cholecystectomy."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
