          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A posterior wall ulcer of the first part of the duodenum is most likely to erode</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A posterior wall ulcer of the first part of the duodenum is most likely to erode which artery, causing life-threatening haemorrhage?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Gastroduodenal artery</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The gastroduodenal artery lies immediately posterior to the first part of the duodenum. Posterior wall ulcers erode into it causing massive haemorrhage, whereas anterior wall ulcers tend to perforate into the peritoneal cavity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Superior mesenteric artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The superior mesenteric artery relates to the third part of the duodenum (causing SMA syndrome), not the first part. The gastroduodenal artery is the vessel intimately related to the posterior wall of the duodenal cap.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Left gastric artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach and has no direct relation to the posterior duodenal wall. The gastroduodenal artery is the at-risk vessel here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Splenic artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The splenic artery runs along the superior border of the pancreas towards the spleen. It is the gastroduodenal artery that lies behind the first part of the duodenum and is eroded by posterior wall ulcers.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the anatomical relation: the gastroduodenal artery lies directly behind the first part of the duodenum. Anterior ulcers perforate; posterior ulcers bleed from this artery."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. This anterior-perforation, posterior-haemorrhage pattern is a cornerstone of duodenal ulcer surgery."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
