          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In portal hypertension, caput medusae develops because of dilation of which vein</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In portal hypertension, caput medusae develops because of dilation of which veins that course within the falciform ligament?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Para-umbilical veins</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Para-umbilical veins run within the falciform ligament, linking the portal system to the epigastric veins of the anterior abdominal wall. Their dilation under raised portal pressure produces the characteristic radiating venous pattern around the umbilicus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inferior epigastric veins</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The inferior epigastric veins are the systemic-side drainage endpoint on the abdominal wall. The veins within the falciform ligament that connect the portal system to these epigastric veins are the para-umbilical veins.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Left gastric vein</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The left gastric vein connects to the oesophageal venous plexus, producing oesophageal varices at the lower oesophagus. Caput medusae involves the para-umbilical veins in the falciform ligament, a different anastomotic site.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Superior rectal vein</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The superior rectal vein anastomoses with the middle and inferior rectal veins at the anorectal junction, producing rectal varices. The umbilical sign (caput medusae) involves the para-umbilical veins.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the five portosystemic sites: at the umbilicus, para-umbilical veins within the falciform ligament connect the portal system to the epigastric veins, producing caput medusae when dilated."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The falciform ligament carries para-umbilical veins — the portal-to-systemic bridge that produces caput medusae when portal pressure exceeds 12 mmHg."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
