          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Right shoulder tip pain following splenic rupture (Kehr's sign) occurs because s</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Right shoulder tip pain following splenic rupture (Kehr's sign) occurs because subdiaphragmatic irritation is referred via which nerve?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Phrenic nerve (C3, C4, C5)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The phrenic nerve provides central sensory supply to the diaphragm. Irritation (by blood or pus) travels via C3–C5 and is referred to the C4 dermatome, which includes the shoulder tip. Mnemonic: C3, C4, C5 keep the diaphragm alive.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Vagus nerve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The vagus nerve passes through the oesophageal hiatus at T10 and supplies abdominal viscera parasympathetically. Shoulder tip pain from diaphragmatic irritation is carried by the phrenic nerve (C3–C5), not the vagus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lower intercostal nerves (T7–T11)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The lower intercostal nerves supply sensory fibres to the peripheral diaphragm and refer pain to the abdominal wall, not the shoulder. Central diaphragmatic irritation causing Kehr's sign travels via the phrenic nerve (C3–C5).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Long thoracic nerve (C5–C7)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. The long thoracic nerve supplies serratus anterior and causes winging of the scapula when injured. Shoulder tip pain from subdiaphragmatic irritation is referred via the phrenic nerve, which shares the C4 dermatome with the shoulder.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The phrenic nerve (C3, C4, C5) supplies sensation to the central diaphragm. Irritation is referred to the C4 dermatome — the shoulder tip. This mechanism underlies Kehr's sign in splenic rupture and subphrenic abscess."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. Understanding that the phrenic nerve (C3–C5) carries referred shoulder tip pain from diaphragmatic irritation explains Kehr's sign in splenic rupture and subphrenic abscess."
                }
              ],
